  "landfallEstimate": "Movement towards Odisha and weaken into a Deep Depression",
  "rawBulletin": "REGIONAL SPECIALISED METEOROLOGICAL CENTRE -TROPICAL CYCLONES,\nNEW DELHI TROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO 30\nDEMS-RSMC TROPICAL CYCLONES NEW DELHI DATED 20.11.2026\nFROM: RSMC \u2013TROPICAL CYCLONES, NEW DELHI\nTO: STORM WARNING CENTRE, NAYPYITAW (MYANMAR)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, BANGKOK (THAILAND)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, COLOMBO (SRILANKA)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, DHAKA (BANGLADESH)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, KARACHI (PAKISTAN)\nMETEOROLOGICAL OFFICE, MALE (MALDIVES)\nOMAN METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT,\nMUSCAT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nYEMEN METEOROLOGICAL SERVICES,\nREPUBLIC OF YEMEN (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nNATIONAL CENTRE FOR METEOROLOGY, UAE (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nPRESIDENCY OF METEOROLOGY AND ENVIRONMENT,\nSAUDI ARABIA (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nIRAN METEOROLOGICAL ORGANISATION, (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nQATAR METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nTROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO. 30 FOR THE NORTH INDIAN OCEAN\n(THE BAY OF BENGAL AND THE ARABIAN SEA) VALID FOR THE NEXT 168\nHOURS ISSUED AT 0600 UTC OF 20.11.2026 BASED ON 0300 UTC OF\n20.11.2026.\nSub: Cyclonic Storm \u201cAkvan\u201d [Pronunciation: Akvan] crossed north Andhra Pradesh\n& south Odisha coasts close to Gopalpur.\nThe Cyclonic Storm \u201cAkvan\u201d [Pronunciation: Akvan] over the westcentral Bay of Bengal near north\nAndhra Pradesh & south Odisha coasts moved north-northwestwards with the speed of 26 kmph\nduring past 06 hours and crossed the north Andhra Pradesh & south Odisha coasts close to Gopalpur\n(near latitude 19.1 & longitude 84.8) with wind speed of 70-80kmph gusting to 90kmph during\n0200-0300 UTC, lay centred over south coastal Odisha & adjoining north coastal Andhra Pradesh\nclose to Gopalpur near Latitude 19.2\u00b0N and Longitude 84.8\u00b0E, at 0300 UTC of today, the 20th\nNovember, 2026, about 120 km northeast of Kalingapatnam (43105,Andhra Pradesh), 130 km west-\nsouthwest of Puri (43053,Odisha), and 230 km northeast of Visakhapatnam (43149,Andhra\nPradesh).\nIt is likely to move nearly north-northeastwards across Odisha and weaken into a Deep Depression\nduring next 03 hours and into a Depression during subsequent 06 hours.\nThe system is under continuous surveillance of the Doppler Weather Radar at Gopalpur.\nCloud distribution: (a) Isolated: <25%, Scattered:25-50%, Broken: 51-75%, Solid:>75%, Convection Intensity: (a) Weak:\nCloud Top Temperature(CTT)>-25\u00b0C, (b) Moderate: CTT:-25\u00b0Cto-40\u00b0C, (c) Intense: CTT: -41\u00b0Cto -70\u00b0C and (d) Very\nIntense::Less than -70\u00b0C Probability of cyclogenesis (formation of depression) :NIL:0%, LOW:1-33%, MODERATE:34-\n66% and HIGH:67-100%\nThe forecast location and intensity of the system is given below:\nDate/Time Position Maximum sustained surface Category of cyclonic\n(UTC) (Lat. 0N/ long. 0E) wind speed (Kmph) disturbance\n20.11.26/0300 19.1/84.8 65-75 gusting to 85 Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/0600 19.9/84.8 55-65 gusting to 75 Deep Depression\n20.11.26/1200 21.5/84.8 25-35 gusting to 45 Depression\n20.11.26/1800 23.0/85.0 15-25 gusting to 35 Well Marked Low\nPressure Area\nAs per INSAT 3DS, vortex (AKVAN) lay over the North Andhra Pradesh and South Odisha\nCoasts centered near 19.0N/84.7E overland. Associated scattered to broken low and\nmedium-level clouds, with embedded intense to very intense convection lay over North Bay\nand Odisha, Gangetic West Bengal and Bangladesh (minimum Cloud-Top Temperature\n(CTT) ranges from -70\u00b0C to -90\u00b0C) and moderate to intense convection over extreme North\nCostal Andhra Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand and Bihar, Sub-Himalayan West Bengal\nNortheast states (minimum CTT) ranges from -40\u00b0C to -70\u00b0C).\nThe estimated central pressure is 1002 hPa and maximum sustained wind speed is 35 kt\ngusting to 45 kt. High to very high Sea condition is prevailing over Bay of Bengal.\nRemarks:\nMadden Julian Oscillation (MJO) is in phase 4 with amplitude more than 1. It is likely to\nphase 4 till 21st November. Thus, MJO is favorable for enhancement of convective activity\nover Bay of Bengal till 21st November. However, the decreasing amplitude of the MJO would\nbe reducing the support. Guidance from NCICS",
